Reawakening OS 0.3.0-r11 — shutdown asks first, a quick panel that closes, Jellyfin, Cockpit

file:   reawakening-os-0.3.0-r11-amd64.iso
size:   1107296256 bytes
sha256: 7de10aa35bdd678f3053cfcca518822acaf086ae02f839d97af91ebcd641ed59

The two headline defects from r10 are gone:

- Shutting down or restarting asks first. The power key, the quick panel's
  Shut down and Restart buttons and the settings panel all raise the same modal
  card ("Shut down Reawakening OS?"), Cancel is focused, Escape cancels, a click
  outside the card does nothing, and nothing happens to the machine until the
  destructive button is chosen. The session takes a low-level handle-power-key
  inhibitor so logind leaves the key alone and it reaches the compositor; the
  inhibitor is released when the compositor exits, so the power key still
  switches a machine with no desktop off, and holding it still cuts the power at
  the hardware level.

- The quick panel closes when it should: a click anywhere outside the card, on
  the desktop or on an application window, dismisses it; Escape dismisses it; the
  gear toggles it; the X button is still there; and a slider being dragged is
  never interrupted. It does this by covering the output with a transparent
  layer surface and drawing the card in the corner, which is also why it can hold
  the keyboard while it is open.

Also in this image:

- Brave keeps its own titlebar (the compositor's server-decoration rule for it
  was tried and is not in this build), with Alt+F10 added alongside Super+F and
  Super+Up for maximize and F11 for true full-screen.
- The pointer theme is named explicitly (Adwaita, shipped in
  adwaita-icon-theme) so the cursor cannot be left as a text bar or a resize
  arrow after an interaction.
- Chromebook top-row keys: a hwdb entry maps the atkbd scan codes 0x40-0x44 to
  brightness down/up, mute and volume down/up on Google machines, and
  refurb-keys handles the F-key fallback. Both paths go through refurb-control,
  so the keys, the bar and the sliders agree.
- Zombie processes are reaped by a thread in the shell; a full session of
  launching and closing applications leaves 0 `Z` entries.
- Jellyfin Media Player 1.13 (Qt6 + libmpv, from Jellyfin's own trixie archive)
  is installed and in the launcher, with Qt6 Wayland, libva and vainfo.
- Cockpit: `refurb-cockpit status|on|off|password` and a remote-access entry in
  the launcher. Off by default and bound to loopback; the toggle needs the
  account password, and the script says so.
- Software is labelled "N applications on this computer · Debian's catalogue"
  with an honest empty state, and searches fall back to descriptions.

Verified in QEMU from this ISO (system emulation, no KVM, no GPU): boots to the
desktop with the bottom bar and wallpaper; serial login as refurb; exactly one
power-key inhibitor held; the power dialog appears from the shell's power
message, Escape cancels it, a click outside it does nothing; the quick panel
opens from the gear, Escape closes it, a click on the Brave window closes it;
Alt+F10 maximizes and keeps the bar; closing Brave from its own close button
removes its taskbar entry; Jellyfin Media Player opens to its server screen;
Cockpit answers on https://127.0.0.1:9090 (200) and the forwarded port from the
host is refused (000), which is the loopback binding doing its job; 0 zombies;
24/24 cargo tests pass; scripts/check-config.sh passes.

Not verified: the Chromebook itself (top row, backlight, speakers and the 160 %
ceiling, video smoothness, the stuck cursor, the hardware power key), a real
radio, Cockpit's login from another computer (the live account has no password),
and the installer, which is deliberately not in this round.
